# Implementation from Spec (Plan Then Execute)

When implementing a feature from a spec:

## Prerequisites
- Spec is committed (`spec:` prefix)
- Tests are committed (`test:` prefix) and currently failing

## Phase 1: Plan (present before writing any code)
1. Run `git diff` against the spec and test commits to see scope
2. Run tests — confirm they fail (TDD "red" phase)
3. Read the full spec and existing code
4. Present the **implementation plan** to the user:
   - Which files will be created or modified
   - What each change does
   - Which failing test(s) each change addresses
   - Any architectural decisions or trade-offs
5. Wait for approval before proceeding

## Phase 2: Execute (after plan approval)
1. Write code following the approved plan
2. Run tests — they should all pass (TDD "green" phase)
3. Self-review: does each new/changed AC have corresponding code?

## Rules
- **Plan first, execute after approval** — never write code without an approved plan
- Build exactly what the spec says — no more, no less
- The tests define "done" — when all tests pass, implementation is complete
- Match existing patterns in neighboring code
- Never weaken a test to make it pass — fix the implementation instead
- For modifications: preserve existing behavior where the spec didn't change
